  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Reviewing the Details**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the specific visual changes requested. I\'ve broken down the instructions into key elements: a subtle green background for the page and alterations to the button. The button\'s text needs modification, the dotted border will become green, and the emoji needs changing to a green arrow pointing upwards. I\'m visualizing the adjustments and considering their impact on the overall aesthetic.\n\n\n**Refining the Emoji Choice**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the best green upward arrow emoji. I\'ve been mulling over options like `\342\206\227\357\270\217`, `\342\254\206\357\270\217`, `\360\237\223\210`, and even considering wrapping a simple HTML arrow in a green span. The goal is to best represent the \"green arrow pointing upwards\" request, so I\'m investigating how these various options render across different platforms. The `\342\254\206\357\270\217` emoji seems promising, but I need to confirm its green appearance in most contexts.\n\n\n**Analyzing CSS Changes**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the CSS alterations, starting with the background. I\'m leaning toward `#f7ffeb` or `rgba(204, 255, 0, 0.08)` for a subtle green. I will start by setting the `background: #f7ffeb;`. Then, I\'m working out the ghost button\'s green dotted border. I think I\'ll change the existing `btn-ghost` class, making the border `2px dotted var(--fire)`. The CSS changes appear straightforward, aside from how to proceed with the social buttons.\n\n\n**Implementing the Style Adjustments**\n\nI\'m now implementing the CSS modifications. I\'m focusing on the button\'s border and social cards. I\'ve decided to alter the existing `.btn-ghost` class, changing the border to `2px dotted var(--fire)`. For social cards, I\'m removing the border entirely using `border: 2px solid transparent;`. I plan to add an `\342\206\227` arrow icon to the right side of the flex container to indicate an external link, using `justify-content: space-between;`. Also I will start by setting the `background: #f9fdf0;` for a very subtle green.\n\n\n**Adjusting the Color Scheme**\n\nI\'m now implementing the color modifications. The background will be a subtle `#f2ffe6`, and I\'ve updated the button\'s dotted border to `#1a9e4b`, after initially exploring using `--fire` (which is `#ccff00`). I\'ve also reevaluated how the social card\'s hover effects should work with the removal of its border, and I\'m leaning toward keeping the transformation effect with a transparent border. I will make the change to `#1a9e4b`. I\'m starting the code changes.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Emoji Choice**\n\nI\'m now implementing the changes to the \"view chart\" button. I\'ve decided to incorporate the user\'s explicit request for a green upward arrow emoji. I\'ll use the Lucide icon `ArrowUp` to ensure a definitively green appearance, overriding the initial plan for a custom SVG. The text will be bolded and styled.\n\n\n**Deciding on the Green Arrow**\n\nI\'m now choosing the best green upward arrow icon. While initially considering emojis like `\360\237\237\251\342\254\206\357\270\217` and custom spans, I\'ve decided to stick with a `lucide-react` icon to maintain consistency. I\'ll use the `ArrowUp` icon, colored `#1a9e4b`, instead of `TrendingUp`, since it\'s cleaner. I\'m also implementing the new `ArrowUpRight` into the social card layout, styling it similarly for a consistent look.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 42
      nanos: 736541291
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
